SEC. 20001: This section appropriates $7.5 billion for fiscal year 2025 to improve military quality of life. Funds are allocated for Marine Corps barracks restoration ($230.48 million), military unaccompanied housing ($1 billion), the Defense Health Program ($2 billion), and a supplemental basic allowance for housing ($2.9 billion). It also provides $590 million to increase the Temporary Lodging Expense Allowance to 21 days, plus funds for tuition assistance, child care, and other support programs. The section temporarily raises the authorized investment percentage for privatized military housing projects from 33⅓% to 60% and extends the authority for privatized military unaccompanied housing through September 30, 2029.

SEC. 20002: This section appropriates $27.5 billion for fiscal year 2025 to enhance shipbuilding capabilities. It funds expansion of the accelerated Training in Defense Manufacturing program ($250 million), next-generation shipbuilding techniques ($492 million), and additional dry-dock capability ($500 million). Major procurements include a second Virginia-class submarine ($4.6 billion), two additional Guided Missile Destroyers ($5.4 billion), and Landing Ship Medium procurement ($1.8 billion). It also supports expansion of unmanned surface and underwater vehicle production, totaling $3.4 billion, and various industrial base and workforce development initiatives.

SEC. 20003: This section appropriates $23.5 billion for fiscal year 2025 to enhance integrated air and missile defense. It funds development of space-based and boost phase intercept capabilities ($5.6 billion), military space-based sensors ($7.2 billion), and military missile defense capabilities ($2.55 billion). For layered homeland defense, it provides $2.2 billion for hypersonic defense systems, $800 million for next-generation intercontinental ballistic missile defense, and $1.975 billion for improved ground-based missile defense radars. It also includes $408 million for Army test range infrastructure in the Indo-Pacific and $530 million for a Missile Defense Agency instrumentation range safety ship.

SEC. 20004: This section appropriates $20.4 billion for fiscal year 2025 to enhance munitions and defense supply chain resiliency. It funds development and production of various missiles, including long-range anti-ship missiles ($780 million), cruise missiles ($938 million), and torpedoes ($600 million). It provides $1 billion for next-generation automated munitions production factories, $2 billion for critical minerals stockpile improvements, and $3.3 billion for Industrial Base Fund grants. It also appropriates $5 billion for critical minerals supply chain investments and $500 million for a capital assistance program with loan authority up to $100 billion.

SEC. 20005: This section appropriates $15.5 billion for fiscal year 2025 to scale low-cost weapons into production. It funds expansion of the small unmanned aerial system industrial base ($1.4 billion), low-cost cruise missiles ($1.5 billion), and Defense Innovation Unit scaling of commercial technology ($2 billion). It provides $1 billion for qualification activities and technical data management, $1.685 billion for military cryptographic modernization, and $250 million for the Quantum Benchmarking Initiative. It also appropriates $1 billion for a capital assistance program with loan authority up to $100 billion.

SEC. 20006: This section appropriates $380 million for fiscal year 2025 to improve Department of Defense efficiency and cybersecurity. It funds business systems replacement ($150 million) and automation/artificial intelligence deployment ($200 million) to accelerate financial statement audits. It provides $10 million for improving the budgetary infrastructure of the Office of the Secretary of Defense and $20 million for DARPA defense cybersecurity programs.

SEC. 20007: This section appropriates $7.9 billion for fiscal year 2025 to enhance air superiority. It funds increased F-15EX production ($3.15 billion), prevents F-22 and F-15E retirements ($488.68 million), and accelerates the Collaborative Combat Aircraft program ($678 million). It provides $400 million to accelerate F-47 production, $750 million for the FA/XX aircraft, and $600 million for Air Force long-range strike aircraft. It also includes $500 million for Navy long-range strike aircraft and various other aircraft production and capability enhancements.

SEC. 20008: This section appropriates $14.4 billion for fiscal year 2025 to enhance nuclear forces. For the Department of Defense, it funds Sentinel intercontinental ballistic missile risk reduction ($2.5 billion), B-21 bomber production expansion ($4.5 billion), and nuclear-armed sea-launched cruise missile development ($2 billion). For the National Nuclear Security Administration, it provides $1 billion for facility construction acceleration, $750 million each for primary and secondary capability modernization, and $540 million for deferred maintenance. It also includes $120 million for domestic uranium enrichment centrifuge deployment and $115 million for nuclear national security missions through artificial intelligence.

SEC. 20009: This section appropriates $12.4 billion for fiscal year 2025 to improve United States Indo-Pacific Command capabilities. It funds military exercises and operations in the Western Pacific ($1.1 billion), infrastructure development ($1.1 billion), and offensive cyber operations ($1 billion). It provides $3.65 billion for military satellites and their protection, $1 billion for the X-37B spacecraft program, and $850 million for replenishment of military articles. It also includes $500 million for economic competition effects, $300 million for mesh network communications, and $200 million for the Guam Defense System program.

SEC. 20010: This section appropriates $16.4 billion for fiscal year 2025 to improve Department of Defense readiness. It funds maritime spares and repair rotable pools ($2.1 billion), Air Force aircraft spares and repairs ($2.118 billion), and Army depot modernization ($1.5 billion). It provides $2 billion for Navy depot and shipyard modernization, $1.64 billion for Special Operations Command equipment and readiness, and $2.5 billion for Air Force facilities sustainment. It also includes $1.5 billion for increased depot and shipyard maintenance activities and $500 million for National Guard unit readiness.

SEC. 20011: This section appropriates $1 billion for fiscal year 2025 to improve Department of Defense border support and counter-drug missions. Funds are for deployment of military personnel in support of border operations, counter-narcotics and counter-transnational criminal organization mission support, operation of national defense areas, and temporary detention of migrants on Department of Defense installations, in accordance with chapter 15 of title 10, United States Code.

SEC. 20012: This section appropriates $10 million for fiscal year 2025 to the Inspector General of the Department of Defense for oversight of activities funded in this title. The funds are to monitor programs with mutual technological dependencies, programs with related data management and data ownership considerations, and programs particularly vulnerable to supply chain disruptions and long lead time components.

SEC. 20013: This section authorizes appropriations for military construction, land acquisition, and military family housing functions for each military department. It requires each military department Secretary to submit a detailed spending plan by project to the Senate and House Armed Services Committees within 30 days of enactment for all funds made available by this title for military construction projects. [end of text]


